.TH CB 1 "2026-11-15" "caveutils" "caveutils Manual"
.SH NAME
cb \- clipboard manager with history
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cb
[\fB\-p\fR] \fBcopy\fR
[\fITEXT\fR...]
.br
.B cb paste
.br
.B cb list
.br
.B cb select
\fIN\fR
.br
.B cb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cb
is a clipboard manager for X11 and Wayland in one program. It keeps a RAM ring
buffer of history (not just the newest entry), owns the clipboard itself so an
old entry can be made current again, and offers a plain CLI and an interactive
TUI.
.PP
The backend is chosen at run time. With
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
set it speaks the data\-control protocol (ext\-data\-control\-v1, else
zwlr_data_control_manager_v1), which needs no focused window and works under dwl
and other wlroots compositors; the clipboard is watched, and the last entry is
kept alive when the app that owned it exits. Otherwise it uses X11 (the ICCCM
CLIPBOARD selection). If the compositor has no data\-control protocol and
.B DISPLAY
is set, it falls back to XWayland.
.PP
Daemon + client, one binary: the first
.BR copy / paste /TUI
invocation starts a background daemon (display connection plus a Unix socket at
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cb.sock ,
or
.I /tmp/.cb\-<uid>.sock
if that is unset); every later invocation is a client of it. All history lives in
RAM in a fixed ring buffer, with no history file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p
Wayland only: when this call starts the daemon, also record the PRIMARY
(middle\-click) selection into the history.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CB_BACKEND
.B wayland
or
.B x11
forces the backend instead of detecting it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B copy \fR[\fITEXT\fR...]
Copy TEXT (or stdin, if no TEXT is given) onto the clipboard.
.TP
.B paste
Print the current clipboard contents.
.TP
.B list
Print the full history, most recent first.
.TP
.B select \fIN\fR
Re\-activate history entry N as the current clipboard contents.
.TP
.B (no args)
Launch an interactive TUI: fuzzy\-search history, Enter to
re\-activate an old entry.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR st (1)
